# Home Assistant specification coverage

Do not read this register as a list of features Home Assistant lacks. Home Assistant already provides device controls, automation editors, schedules, history, account management and recovery flows. The gaps below concern **civica design specifications and acceptance coverage** for those capabilities.

The audience is maintainers and reviewers. The review identified 30 areas needing a dedicated specification or a more complete task contract. Each now has a linked guide with state/capability decisions, complete examples, accessibility and recovery guidance, and integration acceptance recipes. Start with the [Home Assistant catalogue](../home-assistant/index.html). Completion means design coverage. It does not mean a new native adapter or a live device test.

<% audit = JSON.parse(File.read(File.join(root, 'data', 'ha-component-gaps.json'))) %>
<% sources = audit['sources'].to_h { |source| [source['id'], source] } %>

## Read coverage in three parts

| Part | Evidence required |
| --- | --- |
| Native capability | Official Home Assistant documentation or source shows that the feature exists |
| civica implementation | A named maintained component or bounded adapter implements a change; theme variables alone are a narrower claim |
| Design specification | The guide explains capability, state, content, accessibility and failure behaviour, with task-specific acceptance checks |

The current companion adapts known shell hosts, profile colour settings, numeric attention badges, seven native Energy graph types and three named household cards. It also supplies action links. The maintained lighting card adds its own room and scene controls. Other native surfaces keep their own behaviour and the styles they expose. See [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html) for the exact boundary.

## Priority and dependencies

**P1** means the specification is needed before claiming reliable coverage of consequential actions, permissions, privacy, recovery or task accessibility. **P2** covers the remaining work needed for coherent household use. They are priorities for design work. They do not claim that every native surface has a confirmed defect.

Start with the shared state and action contracts, then the domain controls and flows that depend on them. Build native task examples from actual capabilities and synthetic data. Keep a supported native route available while you specify and test a new recipe.

The [machine-readable gap register](../reference/ha-component-gaps.json) contains exact file and line evidence, source records, dependencies, rule-file hashes and acceptance criteria. The [component organisation review](component-audit.html) identifies where shared rules should live.

